WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.490
Ever since the Looper channel went live in
January 2016, there is one video fans have

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.160
been requesting over and over: the untold
truth of Looper.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.110
Well, that moment has finally come, because
we're here to peel back the veil of secrecy

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.970
and give you an inside look at just what went
into creating Looper.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.070
So, here it is, at long last:

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.020
The untold truth of Looper.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.419
​Die Hard try hard

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:32.950
In this time-travel action film, Joseph Gordon-Levitt
and Bruce Willis play young and older versions

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:37.520
of the same character, an assassin named Joe
who's ordered to kill his future self.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.600
Star Joseph Gordon-Levitt faced a tall task
when it came to playing the younger version

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.980
of Bruce Willis, starting with the fact that
they look absolutely nothing alike.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.570
In fact, the film's makeup artists actually
thought it was an impossible task and almost

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.050
quit the film entirely.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:57.160
Director Rian Johnson convinced them to stay,
and after months of experimenting, they finally

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.609
came up with a mix of prosthetics and makeup
that worked — and also took three hours

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:02.960
a day to apply.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.160
That's only one of the lengths Gordon-Levitt
went to in order to deliver an authentic performance,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.160
though.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.790
He religiously studied Willis's performances,
but he was careful to only study newer Willis

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.050
films, rather than classics like Die Hard.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:19.150
Because he wanted to make sure he wasn't playing
a young Willis, but rather a younger version

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.390
of the older Willis.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.680
Geez, even the behind the scenes preparation
for the story is too confusing to understand...

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.600
​The four page script

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.650
While the film hit theaters in 2012, Johnson
actually came up with the idea for Looper

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.310
ten years earlier, back in 2002.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:38.810
At first, it was supposed to be a short film,
and Johnson worked out his initial idea in

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.530
a little four-page draft, which didn't include
most of the twistier thriller aspects of the

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.530
finished film.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.590
But the heart of Joe's story is all there,
something curious fans can see for themselves,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.200
as the story is posted online.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.660
Thank Christopher Nolan

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.960
Director Christopher Nolan's twisty dream
thriller Inception opened two years before

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.160
Looper and became a major critical and commercial
hit.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.340
According to Johnson, Inception was a "revelation"
for him.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.900
He saw it as proof that a wide audience would
accept a complex sci-fi movie if it's made

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:08.900
well.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.560
Inspired, Johnson dug that short story out
of the drawer it had been languishing in for

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.950
years and developed it into the full script
for Looper, eventually signing on his old

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.349
friend and Inception actor Gordon-Levitt to
headline the project.

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:22.990
That's what we call "closing the loop."

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.599
Low-fi sci-fi

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:28.799
There's a ton of cool science fiction stuff
in Looper, like flying bikes, but there was

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:29.909
almost a lot more.

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:33.700
Johnson revealed that he often had to ask
the special effects team to tone down their

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.239
designs because he wanted the future to look
as realistic and plausible as possible.

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:39.430
He told Time magazine,

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:43.990
"As a sci-fi geek, it took a lot of discipline
to say, 'Well that looks really cool, but

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.010
let's pull that way back.'"

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:47.849
The diner was too authentic

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:52.489
One of the most memorable scenes in Looper
involves a not-so-friendly meal at an old-timey

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:53.489
diner.

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:57.709
It's here that Old Joe and Young Joe finally
get a little face time, allowing Gordon-Levitt

00:02:57.709 --> 00:02:59.800
to learn a thing or two about table manners.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:09.200
The diner is cool, but even cooler is the
fact that the whole thing was built from the

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:13.040
ground up on location in Louisiana just for
those scenes.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.400
But the finished set was so authentic, locals
kept interrupting the construction to ask

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.680
when the diner would open.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:22.889
Sadly, you'll need time travel yourself to
visit it, because the fake diner was reportedly

00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:25.400
torn down in 2014.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.180
No family values

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.470
If you watched the trailers and thought Looper
was a tense, sci-fi thriller, you were only

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.470
half right.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:36.099
The heart of the story involves a little boy
who may or may not grow up to be a terrible

00:03:36.099 --> 00:03:37.340
villain in the future.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.920
But you wouldn't know that from the marketing
campaign, which didn't mention the family

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:41.999
drama at all.

00:03:41.999 --> 00:03:46.640
Johnson said Sony shied away from those aspects
because it's a bit harder to sell those elements

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.700
in a poster or two-minute trailer.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.579
A trailer that didn't actually give away the
entire story?

00:03:51.579 --> 00:03:54.529
The future might be worth living in after
all!

00:03:54.529 --> 00:03:56.370
From Paris, with love

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.840
Everybody wants to travel the world, even
futuristic hit men, and Joe eventually winds

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:01.840
up in China.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.590
That was a last minute change, though.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:07.980
The script actually called for Joe to end
up in Paris, but after crunching the numbers,

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.939
Johnson realized they didn't have the budget
to film in France.

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:15.439
That's when the film's Chinese distributor,
DMG, offered to foot the bill if they filmed

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:17.090
those scenes in China instead.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.239
Johnson agreed, and the rest is…well, history.

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:22.450
​Loopholes

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.470
Having someone "close their own loop" is a
critical part of the film's world-building,

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:30.050
but it's not something Johnson explicitly
explained in the film itself.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.400
It wasn't a plot hole, though.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:36.900
In fact, early drafts of the script included
a whole scene where Abe, played by Jeff Daniels,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.610
explains that people in the future are desperately
trying to protect the timeline from being

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.980
messed up by, you know, immoral thugs wielding
guns.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.270
Johnson explained the backstory as to why
the assassins have to close their own loops:

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:51.880
"People in the future, all they know about
time travel is to be afraid of it.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.050
So they’re trying to keep it as tight as
possible.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:58.110
So the initial reason they set it up this
way was to keep the causality loop as tight

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:02.270
as possible [...] Every bit of evidence is
gone from that loop when you kill yourself."

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:07.170
But as good as that explanation is, it's not
hard to prefer Old Joe's disinterest in talking

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.170
about time travel:

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:10.540
"I don't want to talk about time travel s---.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.330
'Cause if we start talking about it, then
we're gonna be here all day talking about

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.070
it—and making diagrams with straws.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.070
Good call.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.490
Look at all these things!

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.500
No one's got time for all that nonsense.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.260
Not even the Doctor wants to get into all
the dirty details…

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:28.640
"People assume that time is a strict progression
of cause to effect, but actually from a non-linear,

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:35.300
non-subjective viewpoint, it's more like a
big ball of wibbly-wobbly, tiny-winy stuff."

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:36.300
Thanks for watching!

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.060
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.410
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

